FAANG / Product-Based Questions
66. Why is Exception immutable?

To preserve consistent error information once created.

67. Why does Java separate Error and Exception?

Errors represent unrecoverable JVM/system failures, while Exceptions represent conditions applications can often handle and recover from.

68. Why does Java have Checked Exceptions?

To force developers to acknowledge and handle recoverable conditions such as file I/O or database access.

69. Why is RuntimeException unchecked?

Because these exceptions usually indicate programming bugs (like NullPointerException or ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException) that should be fixed rather than forced into try-catch blocks.

70. Why shouldn't we catch Exception everywhere?

Because it hides the real cause of failures, can swallow important exceptions, and makes debugging harder. Catch the most specific exception you can reasonably handle